01_07_02_09_23	Debug: {'system_prompt': '\n        You are a Fight The Landlord (Dou Dizhu) player. \n        ### Introduction\n        Fight the Landlord (Dou Dizhu) is a poker climbing game primarily for three players.\n        ### Players, Cards and Deal\n        This game uses a 54-card pack including two jokers, red and black. The cards rank from high to low:\n        red joker, black joker, 2, A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3. Suits are irrelevant.\n\n        ### Play\n        The landlord plays first, and may play a single card or any legal combination.\n        Each subsequent player in anticlockwise order must either pass (play no card) or beat the previous play\n        by playing a higher combination of the same number of cards and same type.\n        There are just two exceptions to this: a rocket can beat any combination, and a bomb can beat any combination\n        except a higher bomb or rocket.\n\n        ### Combinations\n        1. Single card: ranking from three (low) up to red joker (high).\n        2. Pair: two cards of the same rank, from three (low) up to two (high).\n        3. Triplet: three cards of the same rank.\n        4. Triplet with an attached card: a triplet with any single card added.\n        5. Triplet with an attached pair: a triplet with a pair added.\n        6. Sequence: at least five cards of consecutive rank, from 3 up to ace. Twos and jokers cannot be used.\n        7. Sequence of pairs: at least three pairs of consecutive ranks, from 3 up to ace. Twos and jokers cannot be used.\n        8. Sequence of triplets: at least two triplets of consecutive ranks from three up to ace.\n        9. Sequence of triplets with attached cards: an extra card is added to each triplet.\n        10. Sequence of triplets with attached pairs: an extra pair is attached to each triplet.\n        11. Bomb: four cards of the same rank. Beats everything except a rocket and a higher bomb.\n        12. Rocket: a pair of jokers. The highest combination, beats everything.\n        13. Quadplex set: a quad with two single cards OR two pairs attached.\n        Use the EXACT card symbols provided in the Valid Moves list.\n        Prepare a valid JSON object like {"cards": ["♠3", "♥3"]} for your answer, along with your explanation and reason. If you pass, output: {"cards": []}\n        \n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output the action with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n    ', 'user_prompt': "You are playing as a peasant. Your aim is to be the play out all your cards before the landlord does, OR help your teammate (the other peasant) play out their cards before the landlord does. If the landlord plays out all cards first, you lose.\nYou are a Peasant. The Previous Player (Left) is the Landlord (ENEMY). The Next Player (Right) is your TEAMMATE (Peasant). You must cooperate with your teammate to beat the Landlord. The objective is to empty your hand before the landlord does, or help your teammate empty their hand before the landlord does.\n\nYour Hand: ♦5 ♣6 ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 ♥9 ♣9 ♦9 ♦10 ♣J ♦J ♥Q ♠K ♣K ♠A ♠2 RedJoker\nLast Play to Beat: ♠4 ♥4 ♣4 ♣10\n--- Game History (Recent) ---\nTurn 1 (Prev Player): ♠4 ♥4 ♣4 ♣10\n--- Player Info ---\nCards Held by Next Player (Right) [Peasant (Teammate)]: 17\nCards Held by Prev Player (Left) [Landlord (Opponent)]: 16\n--- Cards Remaining Unknown (Card Counting) ---\n(Format explanation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nBlackJoker: 1, 2: 3, A: 3, K: 2, Q: 3, J: 2, 10: 2, 9: 1, 8: 4, 7: 1, 6: 3, 5: 3, 4: 1, 3: 4\nValid Moves:\ntriple_with_single: ♦5 ♠7 ♥7 ♣7\ntriple_with_single: ♣6 ♠7 ♥7 ♣7\ntriple_with_single: ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 ♣9\ntriple_with_single: ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 ♦10\ntriple_with_single: ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 ♣J\ntriple_with_single: ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 ♥Q\ntriple_with_single: ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 ♣K\ntriple_with_single: ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 ♠A\ntriple_with_single: ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 ♠2\ntriple_with_single: ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 RedJoker\ntriple_with_single: ♦5 ♥9 ♣9 ♦9\ntriple_with_single: ♣6 ♥9 ♣9 ♦9\ntriple_with_single: ♣7 ♥9 ♣9 ♦9\ntriple_with_single: ♥9 ♣9 ♦9 ♦10\ntriple_with_single: ♥9 ♣9 ♦9 ♣J\ntriple_with_single: ♥9 ♣9 ♦9 ♥Q\ntriple_with_single: ♥9 ♣9 ♦9 ♣K\ntriple_with_single: ♥9 ♣9 ♦9 ♠A\ntriple_with_single: ♥9 ♣9 ♦9 ♠2\ntriple_with_single: ♥9 ♣9 ♦9 RedJoker\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '**The Situation Unfolds**\n\nOkay, here\'s the deal. I\'m in the Peasant role, sitting between the Landlord (to my left) and my teammate (to my right). I\'ve got a decent hand - a Red Joker, a deuce, a few Aces, Kings and Jacks, two triplets (7s and 9s), and a handful of other cards. The Landlord just tossed out a 4-4-4 with a 10 kicker – a pretty weak play, honestly. It\'s early in the game (turn 1).\n\nMy immediate thought is, "This is my chance!" Given my position, I have the opportunity to block this low play or allow it. I need to prevent the Landlord from getting away with such a weak hand so early on. If I can take control here, I can help my teammate, force the Landlord to play bigger, and set the tempo.\n\nI consider the options. Passing is a non-starter. It lets the Landlord keep the initiative, which is bad for my team. My teammate has 17 cards, same as me, and if they have to beat this, they may break their structure, so it\'s best for me to handle this, as the "gatekeeper".\n\nThe Landlord\'s play is a low triplet, 4-4-4, so it\'s an easy win for me. I have two triplets to play, 7-7-7 and 9-9-9. Playing 7-7-7 beats it, and 7-7-7 is a lower card than 9-9-9, so I shouldn\'t waste 9-9-9 yet. My plan is to beat this with 7-7-7. Then I still have 999 to use, as well as high cards. Since it\'s a 4 with a 10 kicker, I just need to play a single. I have a 5 and a 6. 5 is lower.\n\nSo, the optimal play here is 7-7-7 with a 5 kicker (♦5 ♠7 ♥7 ♣7). This clears a triplet and a small card, and keeps my valuable high cards (Red Joker, 2, A) for later in the game. It is aggressive, which is good. My hand is strong and I can take the lead. I have the power to do it. Let\'s do it.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord has opened with a weak triplet combination (4-4-4-10). As the "gatekeeper" peasant (playing immediately after the landlord), my role is to block small cards to force the landlord to play higher or to seize initiative. I have a very strong hand containing the Red Joker, a 2, an Ace, pairs of K and J, and two triplets (7s and 9s). Passing here would be passive and would force my teammate to respond, potentially breaking their hand or letting the landlord play again. Taking the lead now allows me to control the game, likely playing out my other triplet or utilizing my pairs.\n\nReason: I should beat the landlord\'s low triplet to prevent them from gaining easy momentum. 7-7-7 is my lowest triplet capable of beating 4-4-4. ♦5 is my lowest single card, making it the ideal kicker to attach. This play preserves my higher triplet (9-9-9) and my strong control cards (Red Joker, 2, A) while efficiently clearing low cards from my hand.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♦5", "♠7", "♥7", "♣7"]}'}
